<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-09-15.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="023343"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023343" label="[02:33:43]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; Written an M0 implementation in awk <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/cosinusoidally/tcc_bootstrap_alt/blob/dev/m2min/m0.awk">https://github.com/cosinusoidally/tcc_bootstrap_alt/blob/dev/m2min/m0.awk</a>  . Currently only works in mawk, but I'll fix gawk at some point. Next I'll look at writing a hex2.awk</span></div><div class="line" id="023432"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023432" label="[02:34:32]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; That's probably the 4th awk program I've ever written so might not be the most idiomatic awk code.</span></div><div class="line" id="043835"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#043835" label="[04:38:35]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">cosinusoidally: interesting; if I am reading this correct your get_tok function is doing the wrong thing if given %label</span></div><div class="line" id="044232"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#044232" label="[04:42:32]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(looks like I was wrong, &gt;.&lt; guess I am bad at reading awk scripts ;-p)</span></div><div class="line" id="044601"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#044601" label="[04:46:01]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but you got the byte order wrong; %0x12345678 would be 78 56 34 12 on x86 (which I am assuming is your target architecture) unless the goal was to target knight which is Big Endian not Little byte endian.</span></div><div class="line" id="131504"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#131504" label="[13:15:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; oriansj: in signed_int_to_hex there is code to convert hex literals into little endian hex strings. The t3 contains the hex digits, the order is then flipped by d1= t3[7] t3[8] t3[5] t3[6] t3[3] t3[4] t3[1] t3[2];</span></div><div class="line" id="131534"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#131534" label="[13:15:34]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; So in theory 0x12345678 would become 78563412</span></div><div class="line" id="131858"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#131858" label="[13:18:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; mk_m0_awk is slightly messy atm, but it does compare real output from M0 to the output from m0.awk</span></div><div class="line" id="131941"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#131941" label="[13:19:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; which currently matches even when m0.awk processes a 0x.. hex literal</span></div><div class="line" id="142839"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#142839" label="[14:28:39]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: would it be feasible to write an awk interpreter in M2-compatible C with enough functionality to kick off the awk-based tcc bootstrap?</span></div><div class="line" id="153148"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153148" label="[15:31:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: why wouldn't it be feasible...</span></div><div class="line" id="153155"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#153155" label="[15:31:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though of course that's x86 only</span></div><div class="line" id="172502"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#172502" label="[17:25:02]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: why not, we have unxz, ungz and with just a little bit of effort you should be able to get an awk in mescc-tools-extras</span></div><div class="line" id="182743"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#182743" label="[18:27:43]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hus far we have learned, basically anything can be written in the M2-Planet C subset and will work (if one is willing to do the work)</span></div><div class="line" id="194920"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#194920" label="[19:49:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, M2 subset is not that small anymore</span></div><div class="line" id="194931"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#194931" label="[19:49:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and some of the missing things can be workarounded</span></div><div class="line" id="194947"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#194947" label="[19:49:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you are writing your own code rather than compiling existing codebase</span></div><div class="line" id="230529"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230529" label="[23:05:29]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: fair enough; in M2-Planet we slowly grew from around 1Kloc to 15Kloc</span></div><div class="line" id="230604"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230604" label="[23:06:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, though I guess it's not easy to extend it much more</span></div><div class="line" id="230619"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230619" label="[23:06:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">actually, what are the biggest things we are missing?</span></div><div class="line" id="230628"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230628" label="[23:06:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you added switch/case last I think</span></div><div class="line" id="230633"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230633" label="[23:06:33]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yep</span></div><div class="line" id="230653"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230653" label="[23:06:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, increment/decrement is missing, though it's not that much of a deal if you write your own code</span></div><div class="line" id="230702"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230702" label="[23:07:02]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just like a dozen minor C features one needs to directly build TCC</span></div><div class="line" id="230720"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230720" label="[23:07:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yaeh, tcc needs almost the whole C99...</span></div><div class="line" id="230743"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230743" label="[23:07:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, floats can be patched out, that's what mescc does before building it</span></div><div class="line" id="230846"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230846" label="[23:08:46]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or if one looks at cosinusoidally's work; much less</span></div><div class="line" id="231531"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231531" label="[23:15:31]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as tcc 1.7 didn't have much preprocessor support or even goto support</span></div><div class="line" id="232237"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232237" label="[23:22:37]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or we could let someone fork M2-Planet and (assuming that they get 2-3 people to actively work on extending it) in 2 years it'll probably be able to build GCC directly.</span></div><div class="line" id="232518"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232518" label="[23:25:18]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as that ultimately seems to be the path for every C compiler written.</span></div><div class="line" id="232628"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232628" label="[23:26:28]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(build up to compile the TCC version that can build GCC or build GCC directly)</span></div><br /></div></body></html>